</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Transferability</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A central requirement for practical prompt-based attacks is transferability: in realistic scenarios, attackers can optimize prompts on open-source LLMs where model weights are available, but the true targets are often proprietary or closed-source systems. If an attack prompt generalizes across models, it can be deployed effectively without direct access to the target model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.4 Transferability ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Are LLMs Reliable Rankers? Rank Manipulation via Two-Stage Token Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> evaluates this property by training prompts on Llama-3.1-8B and applying them to several other rerankers. We compare our method (RAF) against the SRP baseline, reporting average rank (lower is better).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our method demonstrates strong transferability: relative to the source model, RAF ranks change only slightly, from <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.12</annotation></semantics></math> on Mistral-7B, <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.55</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.55</annotation></semantics></math> on Deepseek-7B, and even <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.05</annotation></semantics></math> on Vicuna-7B. In contrast, SRP shows larger performance drops, up to <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+1.51" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1.51</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+1.51</annotation></semantics></math> on Deepseek-7B. These results indicate that RAF achieves consistent cross-model effectiveness, while SRP overfits more heavily to the source model’s token preferences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We attribute this robustness to the linguistic naturalness of RAF prompts. Although the generated tokens are based on the loss function of a specific model, the language they compose remains equally natural for other models.
In contrast, alternative approaches tend to select tokens that are highly effective for central models, but these tokens prove ineffective when applied to other models and result in highly unnatural language compositions that are easily detectable.
Qualitative comparisons illustrating this effect are provided in Section <a href="#S4.SS5" title="4.5 Rank Manipulation Analysis ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Are LLMs Reliable Rankers? Rank Manipulation via Two-Stage Token Optimization" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Cross-model transferability on STSData (Camera). Prompts optimized on Llama-3.1-8B are evaluated on other LLM rerankers. RAF maintains consistently lower ranks and smaller cross-model deltas compared to the SRP baseline, highlighting its suitability for real-world deployment where attacks are trained on accessible models but deployed against others.</figcaption>
